## Model Engineering

- Pick better models based on specific benchmarks
  - different models for specific tasks
    - medical understanding : e.g. https://crfm.stanford.edu/helm/medhelm/latest/
    - instructions: https://crfm.stanford.edu/helm/instruct/latest/
      - use prompt optimizer
- LLMs are sensitive to prompt variations
  - data serialization format (csv, json, md, txt+tags, ... ) impacts the training performance
    - LLM-dependent which works best

## Langgraph transition
  - Tutorials:
    - RealPython: https://realpython.com/langgraph-python/
    - Langchain Academy: https://academy.langchain.com/courses/langchain-essentials-python -> https://academy.langchain.com/courses/langgraph-essentials-python -> https://academy.langchain.com/courses/intro-to-langgraph
    - https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/overview
  - App Structure:
    - https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/application-structure#python-pyproject-toml
  - Reference:
    - Langgraph: https://reference.langchain.com/python/langgraph/
  - Langraph deployment server with postgres for persistence and redis for task queue
    - Docs: https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/deployments
    - unreal: connect to server via requests (https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/streaming#curl, https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/add-human-in-the-loop#curl)
      - The Webhook feature seems be an alternative way https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/use-webhooks#curl-2
      - ChatGPT:
        > - With runs/stream or runs/wait/join, your client keeps a connection open (SSE) or polls and then pulls the result.
        > - With webhooks, you give the server a URL once, and the server will POST you the result when the run finishes (event-driven; no long-lived client connections). Webhooks are accepted on several endpoints: POST /threads/{id}/runs, /threads/{id}/runs/stream, /threads/{id}/runs/wait, and the stateless variants POST /runs/stream and /runs/wait. The payload is a Run object. You can even disable webhooks in self-hosted by config.
    - use agent-chat UI for non-game UI: https://github.com/langchain-ai/agent-chat-ui, https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/ui
    - use https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/use-remote-graph for other python/js applications
  - Monitoring UI
    - Langfuse instead of Langsmith:
      - Langraph support: https://langfuse.com/guides/cookbook/integration_langgraph
        - supports low-code prompt managing support (not documented for respective PydanticAI integration)
      - Monitoring
      - Prompt Management (low code)
    - There is also something called "Studio" which could be used? https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/quick-start-studio
      - Seems to work without langsmith for locally deployed graphs (LANGSMITH_TRACING=false)
  - Code:
    - use langchain for agents (https://reference.langchain.com/python/langchain/agents/#langchain.agents.create_agent)
      - could also be used for the "non-fsm / llm-only" approach (just looping nodes)
      - `prebuilt` module (found in some tutorials) seems deprecated
    - user interrupt() for user intput: https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/interrupts, https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/interrupts#validating-human-input
      - API support: https://docs.langchain.com/langsmith/add-human-in-the-loop#curl
    - use add_edge() or Command() for connecting nodes
      - Command [by executing specific nodes on a nodes' return call] adds dependencies directly to nodes, less atomic
  - Future:
    - RAG: https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/agentic-rag, (maybe also see: https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/rag and https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/knowledge-base)
      - pgvector support https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/integrations/vectorstores/index
    - Persistence: https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/persistence
      - might need a way to map user_id+session_id to a corresponding thread_id
      - checkpointing via SQL DBs already implemented: https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/persistence#checkpointer-libraries
  - Questions consumer
    - iterator wont work because non-serializable, might just copy-create a list with remaining questions instead
    - chatgpt:
      ```python
      from typing import TypedDict, Annotated, List, Optional
      from operator import add
      from langgraph.graph import StateGraph, START, END
      # from langchain_core.messages import AIMessage  # if you accumulate messages

      class State(TypedDict):
          questions: List[str]                 # remaining questions
          current_q: Optional[str]             # question being asked now
          answers: Annotated[list, add]        # accumulate answers via reducer

      def ask_next(state: State) -> dict:
          qs = state["questions"]
          if not qs:
              return {}  # nothing to do
          q = qs[0]
          # ... call your model/tool to answer q -> ans ...
          ans = f"stubbed answer to: {q}"
          # consume one question and append answer
          return {
              "current_q": q,
              "questions": qs[1:],
              "answers": [ans],   # reducer `add` appends
          }

      def route(state: State):
          return END if not state["questions"] else "ask"

      g = StateGraph(State)
      g.add_node("ask", ask_next)
      g.add_edge(START, "ask")
      g.add_conditional_edges("ask", route, {END: END, "ask": "ask"})
      graph = g.compile()  # optionally with a checkpointer
    ```
  - !!! No option to host langgraph server without signing up for langsmith, there is an alternative: https://github.com/ibbybuilds/aegra
